Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

EXT. SEATTLE STREET - DAY

Grounded period live-action, shifting from late-1970s Milwaukee to rain-gray Seattle. PETER OLSON, early thirties, lean and brown-haired, walks beneath a gray sky with a paper coffee cup. A neon sign reads TONY'S BOY'S PIZZA.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Many years had passed since my Milwaukee days. Years of pain and growth, years of regret and loss. I was two people: the child in Wisconsin and now the man in Washington.

PETER pauses as a dark-haired woman passes. For an instant, her pale blue eyes are SIONNA'S.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Seven years since my life suddenly stopped being what it was and became what it is now.

EXT. KIRK STREET ALLEY - DAY

Late 1970s. PETER, nine, skinny and bright-eyed, plays kickball with NICK, twelve, already broad-shouldered with reddish-brown hair.

MARK CONNER kicks. The ball goes THROUGH THE WINDOW of MRS. ENGEL'S KITCHEN.

MRS. ENGEL appears behind the broken glass. MARK freezes.

NICK steps forward.

NICK
I'm the one who did it.

A week later, sixty dollars appears in OLIVIA'S hand for the window. NICK says nothing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I lived with Nick and Ma after Dad took off when I was eight. Nick never told Ma it was really Mark. None of us did.

INT. ELEMENTARY SCHOOLYARD - DAY

A memory flashes. PETER and BOBBY laugh near the school. MRS. STARK marches toward them.

NICK steps between them.

NICK
I'll talk to him and Ma will talk to him. He'll never act up in class again. I guarantee it.

Mrs. Stark relents and walks away.

PETER looks up at his brother.

PETER
Thanks, Nick. You saved my skin.

NICK musses his hair.

NICK
I'll always take care of you. Don't forget that.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It made me love him. It made me hate him.

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

Years later. NICK, now a massive, scarred man with a rough face, a scar over his left eyebrow, brown suede jacket, and heavy boots, enters the gym.

A lifter drops a forty-pound barbell and moves aside. Nick takes a fifty-pound bar for warm-ups.

PETER watches, impressed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When Nick walked into a bar or a gym, people took notice. He was a modern-day warrior. I knew he'd stand up for me no matter what.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

A fifth-floor walk-up in a big brick building. NICK climbs the stairs carrying six grocery bags. PETER carries two.

OLIVIA, elderly and nearly blind, sleeps in the bedroom.

NICK
What's Ma doin' back in the bedroom at this time of day?

PETER
I don't know, why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn't be sleeping in the middle of the day like that. That's how old people go downhill.

PETER
I'll do better.

NICK
You don't need a degree to get a good job. All you need is to know somebody.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick worked at the metal factory. I made pizzas. I couldn't afford school because somebody had to stay with Ma. Nick was right. I was too old, too broke, and had to do the best I could with what I got.

EXT. CENTRAL PIZZERIA - DAY

PETER, in a pizza-shop shirt, walks his bicycle toward the door.

SIONNA, pale and slender with short brown hair, light-blue eyes, a fish-symbol necklace, jeans, blue tennis shoes, and a large backpack, trips on the sidewalk and brushes against him.

PETER
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

PETER
I work here. I'm just going to work. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

PETER
That's different. I never heard of that. What does that mean?

SIONNA
My parents named me after an Irish goddess, the River Shannon.

PETER
Do you want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks, I don't eat pizza, but if you want to go have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She gives him her number and walks away. At the corner, she looks back over her shoulder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like I knew her. Like she was looking right through me.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - EVENING

Olivia's pain-pill bottles stand in a careful line on the kitchen counter. She moves one bottle from front to back, then stops, confused.

PETER
Ma, what's the matter?

OLIVIA
I can't find my pain pills. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

PETER
We haven't been to church for over fifteen years. Your pills are right in front of you.

OLIVIA stares at him with glassy eyes, then suddenly remembers.

OLIVIA
Oh, right. I am so sorry.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I hated seeing her like that. I knew I had to tell Nick.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

A Packers game plays above the bar. PETER nurses a beer. NICK enters and slaps his back.

NICK
Hey, little brother. What's shakin'?

PETER
I don't know. Nothin'.

NICK
What's the matter? I can tell you're hidin' something.

PETER tells him about Olivia's confusion.

NICK
I knew she would start going crazy cooped up in that place all day. Why don't you take her to the store or something? Do something with her before she turns into some demented old fart and we have to pay for a nursing home.

PETER
I just needed to get away from her for a while.

NICK
That's more like it.

Through the bar window, PETER sees the Milwaukee Public Library across the street.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
God, sometimes I really hated Nick inside.

INT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

PETER stands beneath old hanging lamps and towering shelves.

PETER
How do I take a book home from here?

A LIBRARIAN gives him a form. He writes his name and address, receives a temporary paper card, and walks to the classic literature shelves.

He pulls out WUTHERING HEIGHTS.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Who needed school when everything was right here, in this building?

INT. PETER'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

PETER reads Wuthering Heights in bed and falls asleep.

In his dream, PETER wears a white shirt, red embroidered tunic, and brown hose. He rides a dappled mare behind SIONNA through a green medieval forest.

In a sunlit field, he helps her down. They embrace and kiss for the first time.

Her eyes are the lightest blue, like early morning.

PETER bolts upright, sweating.

PETER
Hold! What the hell was that?

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was Sionna, but in a different place with old clothes on. I told myself it was make-believe.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

PETER and SIONNA sit at a small table. Both wear backpacks. Students surround them with books and coffee.

SIONNA
The environment of the book is like its people. Wild and unpredictable. A love that never changes will destroy you.

PETER
I feel like I can talk to you about anything.

SIONNA
Maybe you were dreaming about something from a past life.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Something inside of me was coming awake. I wasn't just a guy who made pizzas, took care of Ma, or followed Nick.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

PETER lies awkwardly on a purple mat among twenty practiced students. His shirt has fallen over his face.

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

PETER
Are you kidding me?

SIONNA
Your practice is your practice.

PETER
Yeah, well, my practice is not the best practice.

SIONNA
That's why they call it "practice."

PETER smiles despite himself. At the end of class, he lies still in corpse pose.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Reading Buddhism, I learned that attaching ourselves to people and things causes suffering. We have to be willing to let go.

EXT. MILWAUKEE SIDEWALK - DAY

PETER and SIONNA walk beneath green trees. A few boys laugh at them from a corner.

PETER turns toward the boys, angry. SIONNA calmly keeps walking.

SIONNA
Love is the universal language, and we know how to speak it.

PETER
You're the only one I can speak it to, I guess.

SIONNA
I could never love anyone like I love you.

PETER
I could never love anyone the way I love you either.

They kiss.

INT. DR. CARSON'S OFFICE - DAY

OLIVIA sits outside the office, confused and clutching her chest. PETER faces DR. CARSON.

OLIVIA
It feels like my heart is going to jump out of my chest.

DR. CARSON
Your mother's blood pressure is dangerously high. Take her directly to the emergency room. She needs intravenous medicine until we can stabilize her.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Three days later. A blue weekly medication container sits open on the table.

PETER
Every day has the pills you need. There are separations for morning and night.

NICK enters in his brown suede jacket, face red, hair wild.

NICK
Oh, holy shit! Aren't you Nurse Nancy?

PETER
Sionna helped me figure it out.

NICK
This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

PETER
Stop it, Nick.

NICK
Don't you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

PETER
I'm not a bastard!

NICK slaps him across the head. PETER's ear rings. Olivia watches from her chair.

NICK
Don't you ever talk that way about my mother.

He storms out.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

The bar is crowded. NICK spots LINDSEY with another man near the pool tables.

PETER steps in front of him.

PETER
Nick, leave it. Lindsey can see whoever she wants.

NICK
Keep out of it! You're going to be next if you don't get out of my face.

NICK draws back his fist. PETER ducks as JOE and BOUNCER HARRIS rush in.

Nick head-butts Harris and breaks free.

PETER runs into the freezing night.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

PETER collapses onto SIONNA'S green suede sofa. She strokes his head.

SIONNA
No one does that, you know. No one who really cares about you.

PETER
He does care about me. He's my brother.

SIONNA
He is a controlling ass, and you are showing him that you don't need him.

PETER falls asleep in her lap.

In a dream, SIONNA rises from a lake in white fabric. A giant NICK approaches through the woods with a mace. Sionna spreads her arms. An invisible force drives Nick backward until he shrinks and vanishes.

PETER wakes beside her at three in the morning.

PETER
You were an angel. You protected us from Nick.

SIONNA
Well, that sounds about right. Let's go to bed.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Months pass. College books and pill containers fill the apartment. OLIVIA laughs with SIONNA over a dish of pie.

NICK enters and looks from the pie to Sionna.

NICK
So, you're a university lady. What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA
I have a major in English and a minor in Philosophy. I'm thinking college professor.

NICK
Sometimes all that school just doesn't pay off.

SIONNA
Diversity helps us learn. You can't keep a good woman down.

Nick has no answer.

NICK
I'll take Ma to Mass Saturday.

PETER watches him carefully.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick began taking care of Ma. He brought her treats, laid out her pills, and acted as if nothing had changed.

EXT. ST. PATRICK'S CHURCH - EVENING

NICK helps OLIVIA from the burnt-orange Pontiac Trans Am, its yellow flames and hood eagle bright beneath the church lights.

Three empty Jack-and-Coke glasses rattle on the floorboard.

INT. PONTIAC TRANS AM - NIGHT

NICK drives OLIVIA through Milwaukee. She sits quietly beside him.

EXT. WERNER AND LOWE INTERSECTION - NIGHT

The Pontiac enters the intersection.

A car slams into it broadside.

Glass bursts across the cabin.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MORNING

PETER enters and finds the apartment empty. Olivia's purse and coat are gone.

The answering machine blinks: FIVE MISSED CALLS.

PETER presses PLAY. A sheriff's voice and a hospital nurse leave urgent messages.

PETER grabs the phone.

PETER
Is Ma okay?

He listens, pale and sweating.

INT. MILWAUKEE COUNTY SHERIFF'S STATION - DAY

OFFICER DUPUIS, large and gray-haired with a thin mustache, sits across from PETER.

OFFICER DUPUIS
There has been a serious accident involving your brother, Nicholas Olson, and your mother, Olivia Olson. Your brother is in serious condition at Frodo Hospital. Your mother passed away at the scene.

PETER's hands shake around a paper cup of water.

PETER
Can I call someone? Can I have a ride to the hospital?

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

NICK lies bruised and broken beneath white sheets, oxygen tubing in his nose, one leg immobilized.

PETER stands at the bedside.

NICK
I fucked up, man. I can't believe Ma's gone. I didn't mean to have three Jack and Cokes before I remembered I needed to take her.

PETER
Whose fault was it, Nick?

NICK
You think I meant to kill Ma?

PETER looks at the tubes, the bruises, and the man who once seemed invincible.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ma was dead. And Nick had killed her.

EXT. ST. MARY'S CEMETERY - DAY

A modest funeral ends beneath a cold sky. PETER drops a handful of dirt onto Olivia's coffin. SIONNA stands beside him, one hand on his arm.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The only thing I could do was keep moving. Work, school, Ma's grave, and Sionna.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Months later. NICK, recovered but wild-eyed, stands in Sionna's living room. SIONNA wears loose clothes and her fish necklace.

NICK
You're the reason I'm probably going to jail. If it wasn't for you, we'd all be just fine.

SIONNA
Nick, this has nothing to do with me. Let's talk this out rationally.

NICK
Break it off with him tonight or else. Then you're going to get your hands on some cash.

He grabs her throat and pins her against the wall. With his other hand, he opens a switchblade.

NICK
If I wanted to kill you, I could kill you right now.

The front door slams open. PETER enters.

PETER
Nick!

Nick jerks toward him. The blade strikes Sionna high in the left chest and shoulder. Blood spreads across her shirt.

Nick elbows PETER and flees.

PETER catches Sionna as she collapses and presses his shirt against the wound.

PETER
My brother stabbed her. Send an ambulance.

Sirens approach. The paramedics take over. A monitor begins to beep.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The beeping sound is good. It means she still has a heartbeat.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DAY

SIONNA sits in a chair with her arm immobilized. Her pale face is distant.

PETER kneels before her.

SIONNA
I can't feel me anymore.

PETER
You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
As long as there is any doubt, I can't go on with you. Maybe we just need a break. I'm going to Mom and Dad's.

PETER
Stay here with me and we can work things out.

SIONNA looks at him with tears in her light-blue eyes.

The door closes behind her.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She survived, but the wound inside her stayed open. Nick was sentenced for the stabbing and the drunk-driving death. I finished school, moved west, and built a life without either of them.

EXT. BELLTOWN STREET - DAY

Seven years later. Rain falls around PETER, now a successful business owner in a dark coat. A delivery driver loads boxes marked TONY'S BOY'S PIZZA into a van.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The outer flesh heals. The inner wounds can linger for the rest of our lives. Holding onto resentment kept me in my own prison.

INT. SEATTLE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Candles glow around a dozen meditating students. PETER sits cross-legged, eyes closed.

In his mind, a discarded pizza box lies on a vivid green hillside. A dark-skinned sage in white wraps stands beside it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Stop judging things. No things are neither good nor bad, but thinking makes it so. Start living the truth.

PETER opens his eyes. He writes one word in a notebook:

FORGIVE.

He opens his laptop and books a flight to Milwaukee.

EXT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

PETER stands before the old brick building. The alley is still there, but the dented garbage cans have become green dumpsters. The old pizzeria is now CENTRAL COSMETOLOGY.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I came back as a grown man. Not Nick's little pawn. Not Ma's invisible helper. Just me.

EXT. ST. MARY'S CEMETERY - DAY

PETER places golden mums in the vase at OLIVIA'S grave.

The inscription reads: I HAVE FOUGHT THE GOOD FIGHT. I HAVE FINISHED THE RACE. I HAVE KEPT THE FAITH.

PETER
Yes, you did, Ma.

INT. GREEN BAY PRISON VISITATION ROOM - DAY

PETER sits behind thick glass. NICK, now thin, gray, and lined with age, enters the opposite booth.

They pick up their yellow telephones.

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came.

PETER
I'm here to tell you that even though you are my brother and we've been through a lot of shit together, I am no longer interested in hearing you control my life.

Nick looks down.

PETER
You held me down my entire life. You killed my mother in a drunk-driving accident. You tried to kill the love of my life. You're an asshole, Nick.

Nick's eyes fill.

PETER
But I forgive you. I forgive you because it sets me free. It releases your control over me forever.

NICK
You're right. I did all that shit. I am a huge asshole. You deserve to live out there. I deserve to live in here.

A GUARD signals that time is nearly over.

PETER
After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother. Do you hear me?

NICK
I hear you, man. I love you too.

PETER
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. We'll be expanding. You always said I couldn't do it. I did it, Nick.

Nick smiles through tears.

NICK
President Olson. That's fucking amazing. I am so proud of you, man.

The guard signals again. Nick hangs up and is led away.

PETER remains seated, holding the silent receiver.

INT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT - DAY

PETER waits at the boarding gate. His boarding pass reads PETER OLSON.

NICK'S voice echoes in his memory.

NICK (V.O.)
President Olson.

PETER looks at his name and smiles.

PETER
That's me. President Peter Olson.

FADE OUT.

THE END
